text><text x="600.0" y="340" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="#C85A32" text-anchor="middle" letter-spacing="1">STORE HERE</text><text x="600.0" y="385" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">beside dryer</text><text x="600.0" y="427" font-size="18" font-weight="400" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">dryer balls</text><text x="600.0" y="469" font-size="18" font-weight="400" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">lint brush</text><text x="600.0" y="511" font-size="18" font-weight="400" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">drying rack</text><line x1="701" y1="380" x2="723" y2="380" stroke="#6E645C" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#dshw-a)"/><rect x="726" y="180" width="196" height="400" rx="18" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="726" y="180" width="196" height="120" rx="18" fill="#C85A32"/><rect x="726" y="270" width="196" height="30" fill="#C85A32"/><text x="824.0" y="228" font-size="30" font-weight="700" fill="#FFFFFF" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text x="824.0" y="272" font-size="19" font-weight="700" fill="#FFFFFF" text-anchor="middle">FOLD &amp; HANG</text><text x="824.0" y="340" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="#C85A32" text-anchor="middle" letter-spacing="1">STORE HERE</text><text x="824.0" y="385" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">counter or table</text><text x="824.0" y="427" font-size="18" font-weight="400" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">hanging rod</text><text x="824.0" y="469" font-size="18" font-weight="400" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">hangers</text><text x="824.0" y="511" font-size="18" font-weight="400" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">lost-sock bin</text><line x1="925" y1="380" x2="947" y2="380" stroke="#6E645C" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#dshw-a)"/><rect x="950" y="180" width="196" height="400" rx="18" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="950" y="180" width="196" height="120" rx="18" fill="#6E645C"/><rect x="950" y="270" width="196" height="30" fill="#6E645C"/><text x="1048.0" y="228" font-size="30" font-weight="700" fill="#FFFFFF" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text x="1048.0" y="272" font-size="19" font-weight="700" fill="#FFFFFF" text-anchor="middle">PUT AWAY</text><text x="1048.0" y="340" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="#C85A32" text-anchor="middle" letter-spacing="1">STORE HERE</text><text x="1048.0" y="385" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">by the exit</text><text x="1048.0" y="427" font-size="18" font-weight="400" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">one basket</text><text x="1048.0" y="469" font-size="18" font-weight="400" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">per person</text><text x="1048.0" y="511" font-size="18" font-weight="400" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">back to rooms</text><text x="600" y="640" font-size="21" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">Arrange your stations in this order and laundry never crosses the room twice.</text><text x="600" y="678" font-size="18" fill="#6E645C" text-anchor="middle">No room for a counter? A fold-down table or the top of a front-loading dryer works as station 4.</text></svg><figcaption style="text-align:center;font-size:0.9em;color:#6E645C;">Every supply lives at the station where you use it.</figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You don&#8217;t need a big room for this. In a small laundry room or a closet, the stations just sit closer together. If you have a top-loader, see our guide to <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-laundry-room-ideas-top-loading-washer/">small laundry room ideas with a top loading washer</a> for storage that won&#8217;t block the lid.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Organize a Laundry Room in 6 Steps</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Empty every surface and shelf.</strong> Take everything out so you can see what you actually have.</li>



<li><strong>Declutter.</strong> Toss empty and nearly empty bottles, combine duplicates, and let go of products you never use. Match up orphan socks or give them a deadline.</li>



<li><strong>Clean while it&#8217;s empty.</strong> Wipe shelves and machine tops, and clean behind and around the dryer, where lint builds up.</li>



<li><strong>Map your stations.</strong> Decide where sorting, washing, drying, folding and putting away happen, following the flow above.</li>



<li><strong>Contain supplies by station.</strong> Store each item next to the machine or surface where you use it, in baskets or bins.</li>



<li><strong>Label bins and hampers.</strong> Labels make it possible for everyone in the house to help, not just the person who set it up.</li>
</ol>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-06-before-after.jpg" alt="Laundry room before and after: overflowing baskets and clothes on the floor next to the same room organized with shelves, baskets and sorting bins" class="wp-image-699" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-06-before-after.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-06-before-after-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-06-before-after-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-06-before-after-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">18 Laundry Room Organization Ideas</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Sorting</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-02-sorting-hamper.jpg" alt="Three-section rolling laundry sorting hamper with whites, darks and towels" class="wp-image-703" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-02-sorting-hamper.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-02-sorting-hamper-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-02-sorting-hamper-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-02-sorting-hamper-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>1. Use a three-section sorting hamper.</strong> A hamper with separate bags for lights, darks and towels means clothes are sorted the moment they come in. When one bag is full, that&#8217;s a load.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>2. Keep mesh bags for delicates and socks.</strong> Mesh laundry bags protect delicates and keep socks together in the wash, so fewer end up in the lost-sock pile.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>3. Give each person a basket.</strong> One basket per person for clean clothes makes putting laundry away faster, and older kids can carry their own basket to their room.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Supplies</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-03-supply-shelf.jpg" alt="Laundry room shelf with a glass detergent dispenser, jars of clothespins and dryer balls, and woven baskets" class="wp-image-701" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-03-supply-shelf.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-03-supply-shelf-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-03-supply-shelf-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-03-supply-shelf-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>4. Store supplies at arm&#8217;s reach of the washer.</strong> A shelf above or beside the machines keeps detergent, stain remover and dryer balls exactly where you use them, instead of on top of the washer.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>5. Use a dispenser for liquid detergent.</strong> A glass dispenser with a spout looks tidy and makes pouring easy. Label it clearly so no one mistakes it for something else.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>6. Keep laundry pods high and sealed.</strong> The U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission recommends keeping liquid laundry packets <a href="https://www.cpsc.gov/s3fs-public/390_SafetyAlert_LaundryPackets_112021.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sealed in their original packaging and locked up out of a child&#8217;s sight and reach</a>. The top shelf or a latched cabinet is the right spot.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>7. Put backups in one labeled basket.</strong> Extra detergent, dryer sheets and stain spray go in a single backup basket. Before you buy more, check it.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Folding and Hanging</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-04-folding-station.jpg" alt="Folded towels and shirts on a laundry room counter above front-loading machines used as a folding station" class="wp-image-702" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-04-folding-station.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-04-folding-station-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-04-folding-station-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-04-folding-station-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>8. Create a folding station.</strong> A counter over front-loading machines or a table nearby gives clean laundry a place to land and get folded right away, instead of going back into a basket.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>9. Add a fold-down table if you have no counter.</strong> A wall-mounted fold-down table gives you a folding surface when you need it and folds flat when you don&#8217;t.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-05-hanging-rod.jpg" alt="Laundry room hanging rod with shirts on wooden hangers and a wire basket for lost socks on the wall" class="wp-image-700" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-05-hanging-rod.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-05-hanging-rod-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-05-hanging-rod-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-room-05-hanging-rod-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>10. Install a hanging rod.</strong> A rod near the dryer lets you hang shirts straight out of the machine, which means fewer wrinkles and less ironing. Keep a stack of matching hangers nearby.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>11. Add a lost-sock bin.</strong> A small wall basket collects single socks until their partner turns up. Clear it out once a month.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Walls and Small Spaces</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>12. Use a pegboard for small tools.</strong> A pegboard keeps a lint roller, stain brush, scissors and a spray bottle off the counter and easy to grab.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>13. Slide a slim cart between the machines.</strong> The narrow gap beside a washer or dryer is perfect for a slim rolling cart with everyday supplies.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>14. Put the back of the door to work.</strong> An over-the-door organizer holds cleaning cloths, dryer sheets and small bottles in space that&#8217;s usually empty.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>15. Keep the dryer area clear.</strong> Don&#8217;t pile baskets or supplies against the dryer. The U.S. Fire Administration recommends <a href="https://www.usfa.fema.gov/downloads/pdf/publications/clothes_dryer_fire_safety_flyer.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cleaning the lint filter before and after each cycle</a> and making sure the vent behind the dryer isn&#8217;t crushed or restricted.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Small Laundry Room Organization Ideas</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>16. Go vertical with matching bins.</strong> In a small laundry room, the wall above the machines is the biggest storage space you have. Two or three shelves with same-size bins hold far more than one deep shelf, and labels on the bins keep everyone putting things back. Over a top-loader, keep the shelves about 2 feet above the lid so it can open fully.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>17. Mount a fold-flat drying rack.</strong> A wall-mounted rack that folds against the wall replaces a floor-standing rack that blocks the room. Put it where it won&#8217;t hit a door or the dryer vent, and fold it away as soon as the clothes are dry.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>18. Add pedestal drawers under front-loaders.</strong> Many front-loading washers and dryers have matching pedestals with a drawer, which raise the machines to a more comfortable height and add hidden storage for towels, mesh bags and spare supplies. Use the pedestal made for your model, so the machines stay stable.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If your washer and dryer sit behind doors in a hallway or bathroom, our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/laundry-closet-ideas/">laundry closet ideas</a> cover vented doors, shelves above a stacked pair and storage for tight closets.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The 10-Minute Weekly Laundry Room Reset</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Minutes 1–3:</strong> put every bottle and tool back at its station.</li>



<li><strong>Minutes 4–5:</strong> clear the folding surface and return empty baskets.</li>



<li><strong>Minutes 6–8:</strong> wipe the machine tops and check the lint area around the dryer.</li>



<li><strong>Minutes 9–10:</strong> check the backup basket and add anything that&#8217;s running low to your shopping list.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common Laundry Room Organization Mistakes</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Storing supplies on top of the machines.</strong> They get in the way of every load and can fall behind the machines.</li>



<li><strong>Having nowhere to fold.</strong> Without a folding station, clean clothes go back into baskets and stay there.</li>



<li><strong>Keeping every half-used bottle.</strong> Combine or finish them. One open bottle of each product is enough.</li>



<li><strong>Ignoring the space around the dryer.</strong> Lint and clutter behind the dryer are a safety issue, not just a tidiness one.</li>



<li><strong>Keeping clothes nobody wears.</strong> If the same items keep going through the wash without being worn, a <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/closet-clean-out/">closet clean out</a> will shrink your laundry pile too.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Laundry Room Organization FAQ</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do I organize my laundry room?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Set up five stations in the order laundry moves: sort, wash, dry, fold or hang, and put away. Store each supply next to where you use it, keep the machine tops clear, and add a folding surface and a hanging rod.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What should be stored in a laundry room?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Only what you use for laundry and a small stash of backups: detergent, stain remover, dryer balls or sheets, hangers, mesh bags, a lint roller and a few cleaning cloths. Household overflow belongs somewhere else.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do I organize a small laundry room?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Go vertical with shelves and matching bins above the machines, use the gaps beside them for a slim cart, add a fold-flat drying rack, and use the walls and back of the door for hanging storage. Pedestal drawers under front-loaders add hidden storage too. Our guide to <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-laundry-room-ideas-top-loading-washer/">small laundry room ideas with a top loading washer</a> has more space-saving ideas.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How can I make laundry faster?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sort as you go with a divided hamper, keep supplies at the washer, fold or hang clothes straight out of the dryer, and give each person their own basket to take clothes away.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Short on space? See our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-laundry-room-ideas/">small laundry room ideas</a> for layouts, slim storage and design tricks that make a tiny laundry room work harder.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you are ready to add cabinets, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/laundry-room-cabinet-ideas/">laundry room cabinet ideas</a> for small rooms, tight budgets and stacked or side-by-side machines. To check what fits first, try our free <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/washer-dryer-space-calculator/">washer and dryer space calculator</a>.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The best laundry room organization ideas follow the laundry: sort by the door, supplies at the washer, a place to fold and hang, and baskets ready to go back to each room. Set up the flow once, and the room mostly keeps itself tidy.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Save this guide to Pinterest and start with the one station that frustrates you most.</p>



